#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Render the retained application evidence without rerunning measurements."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,statistics
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2];W=R/'build/app-comparison-20261024'
d=json.loads((W/'results.json').read_text());project=json.loads((W/'project-search.json').read_text());baseline=json.loads((W/'generic-number-baseline/results.json').read_text())
names={'dyn':'Dyn std','gcc':'GCC C + yyjson','clang':'Clang C + yyjson','rust':'Rust + serde_json','go':'Go std','node':'Node std','python':'Python std'}
http={k:{field:statistics.median(x[field] for x in rows) for field in rows[0]} for k,rows in d['http'].items()}
lines=['# Real application workloads: Dyn and other libraries','', 'These are working small app workloads, not ports of complete production products. On this Linux host, Dyn std can implement all three apps without third-party libraries. Performance is mixed; this is not a universal language ranking.','', '## CLI results','', 'Nine rotating samples after two warmups, one CPU, warm filesystem cache, startup and I/O included. Every output is validated. Times are medians.','', '| Implementation | JSONL report ms | Large search ms | Actual project search ms |','|---|---:|---:|---:|']
for k,n in names.items():lines.append(f'| {n} | {d["cli"]["report"]["medians"][k]["seconds"]*1000:.2f} | {d["cli"]["search"]["medians"][k]["seconds"]*1000:.2f} | {project["medians"][k]["seconds"]*1000:.2f} |')
lines+=['', f'JSONL: {d["inputs"]["report"]["records"]:,} records, {d["inputs"]["report"]["bytes"]:,} bytes, including nested data, escapes and Unicode. Large search: {d["inputs"]["search"]["files"]:,} files, {d["inputs"]["search"]["bytes"]:,} bytes. Actual project search: {project["expected"]["files"]:,} source files, {project["expected"]["bytes"]:,} bytes copied from compiler/src and compiler/std without changing contents. All searches count byte lines containing ERROR and ignore symlinks.','', 'C/yyjson and Rust/serde_json lead JSON ingestion. Dyn beats Go/Python on this JSONL case but trails Node. Dyn search trails the other compiled ports. Its lower startup cost helps against Node/Python on the small actual project, but not enough on the large tree.','', '## HTTP/JSON results','', 'Five rounds per std server. Each round validates 500 responses: 100 warmups, 100 single-client samples, then 300 across four clients. One server CPU, separate client CPUs, new connection per request. A request contains 64 records / 13,974 bytes. Throughput includes client validation overhead. Table entries are medians across rounds.','', '| Std server | Single-client median ms | Four-client requests/s | Four-client sample p95 ms | Server peak RSS MiB | Startup ms |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for k,v in http.items():lines.append(f'| {names[k]} | {v["single_median_seconds"]*1000:.3f} | {v["four_requests_per_second"]:.0f} | {v["four_p95_seconds"]*1000:.3f} | {v["peak_rss_kib"]/1024:.1f} | {v["startup_seconds"]*1000:.2f} |')
lines+=['','Dyn is competitive on median latency and throughput in this small service; Node leads throughput. Dyn has worse sampled four-client p95 latency than the other servers, so the throughput result is not an across-the-board service win. Different worker/event-loop designs contribute to the application comparison. C/Rust HTTP frameworks were not selected or measured.','', '## Why the first Dyn port was slower','',f'The original Dyn port read integer fields through json.as(f64). It took {baseline["cli"]["report"]["medians"]["dyn"]["seconds"]*1000:.2f} ms for the JSONL report. The final app uses checked u64 reads for ordinary integer tokens, preserving the f64 fallback for decimal/exponent spellings; it takes {d["cli"]["report"]["medians"]["dyn"]["seconds"]*1000:.2f} ms. Identical schema/oracle checks pass, and an independent review found no semantic change. This is an application API choice, not a compiler/std patch. The all-f64 sources and raw measurements remain under generic-number-baseline/.','', 'Code inspection explains the credible mechanism: the general decimal-to-f64 reader performs big-integer comparisons and a bit-pattern search, even for simple integer text. The app-level substitution isolates the practical benefit of avoiding that path for integer fields; it does not establish how much of the remaining JSON gap belongs to parsing, allocation, field lookup, or numeric conversion.','', '## Is std good enough?','', 'It has enough functionality for these Linux applications and supports low-memory arena reuse. It is not uniformly fast or fully production-qualified by these tests. Concrete limits exposed here:','', '- Untouched lazy Arena{} fails fs.read_all with Capacity; Linux process.arguments inherits the failure. Explicit arena initialization is the app workaround. See std-probe.log and std-probe/main.dyn.','- Directory iteration is Linux-specific; unknown entry types fail explicitly in the Dyn app.','- General decimal-to-f64 conversion needs performance work; using typed integer reads matters.','- File search is behind C/Rust/Go here. Scalar byte search and 4-KiB read chunks are source-level profiling candidates; this comparison has not causally isolated their individual costs.','', '## Validation and limits','',f'{len(d["correctness"])} correctness groups passed: all seven CLI variants and four std HTTP servers. Checks include full-corpus output oracles, escaped keys/values, arbitrary-byte files, empty/final/consecutive lines, symlink exclusions, 12 malformed/schema/overflow cases, stdout failures, HTTP body limits, and clean shutdown. Harness negative controls prove timeout descendant cleanup and bounded invalid/partial readiness handling. App source hashes match before/after the measured run.','', f'The Dyn compiler hash is {d["compiler_sha256"]}. {d.get("product_integrity",{}).get("compared_files",0)} compiler/std/runtime files match the preceding 191-gate qualification manifest. No compiler/std changes, dependency installs or commits were made for this experiment. Benchmark source lives under tools/app-comparison/.','', 'This comparison uses DOM JSON APIs, not typed-deserialization alternatives or tuned production tools. Libraries, allocation strategies, traversal APIs and runtime behavior differ. The valid corpus excludes duplicate-key and invalid-UTF-8 policy differences. Persistent HTTP, TLS, databases, persistence, multi-core scaling, cold storage, platform portability and production robustness are not qualified. Sample percentiles are not latency guarantees. Observed build times include cache effects and are not ranked.','', '## Reproduce and inspect','', 'From compiler/: run tools/app-comparison/check_harness.py, then run.py, project_search.py, and report.py. Dependencies and scope are documented in tools/app-comparison/README.md and SPEC.md. Raw data: results.json, project-search.json, expected.json, run.log, harness-controls.log, std-probe.log and generic-number-baseline/. Versions, commands, hashes, memory and every timing sample are retained.']
(W/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
charts={}
for key,label,data in [('report','JSONL report · 60,000 records',d['cli']['report']['medians']),('search','Text-tree search · 64.3 MB',d['cli']['search']['medians']),('project','Actual project search · 264 files',project['medians'])]:
 charts[key]={'label':label,'note':'Includes startup and warm-cache file I/O. Same checked output for every implementation.','metrics':{'time':{'label':'Wall time · ms · lower is faster','values':{k:v['seconds']*1000 for k,v in data.items()},'higher':False},'memory':{'label':'Peak RSS · MiB · lower is smaller','values':{k:v['rss_mib'] for k,v in data.items()},'higher':False}}}
charts['http']={'label':'HTTP JSON service · four clients','note':'One server CPU; new connection per request. Different std server designs. No C/Rust HTTP framework measured.','metrics':{'rps':{'label':'Requests / second · higher is faster','values':{k:v['four_requests_per_second'] for k,v in http.items()},'higher':True},'latency':{'label':'Single-client median · ms · lower is faster','values':{k:v['single_median_seconds']*1000 for k,v in http.items()},'higher':False},'p95':{'label':'Four-client sample p95 · ms · lower is faster','values':{k:v['four_p95_seconds']*1000 for k,v in http.items()},'higher':False},'memory':{'label':'Server peak RSS · MiB','values':{k:v['peak_rss_kib']/1024 for k,v in http.items()},'higher':False}}}
html='''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#111b24;color:#edf2f6;font:16px system-ui}main{max-width:1060px;margin:auto;padding:30px}.tag{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:2px;color:#8bc8ff}h1{font-size:33px;margin:8px 0}p{line-height:1.5;color:#b2c2cf}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin:22px 0}.card,.panel{padding:20px;border-radius:12px;background:#1c2b37;border:1px solid #334754}.big{font-size:28px;color:#8bc8ff;font-weight:700}.small{font-size:13px;color:#b2c2cf}.selectors{display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}select{font:15px system-ui;background:#293e4d;color:white;border:1px solid #58778c;padding:9px;border-radius:6px}h2{font-size:21px}.row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:175px 1fr 90px;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin:13px 0;font-size:14px}.track{height:20px;background:#101d27;border-radius:4px}.bar{height:100%;background:#7b91a3;border-radius:4px}.row.dyn .bar{background:#8bc8ff}.row.dyn{font-weight:700}.value{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.notes{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;margin-top:20px}.notes strong{color:#f0d4aa}.footer{margin-top:20px;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid #334754;font-size:12px;color:#a3b7c7;line-height:1.5}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:18px}.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr}.notes{grid-template-columns:1fr}.row{grid-template-columns:115px 1fr 65px}h1{font-size:27px}}</style></head><body><main><div class="tag">APPLICATION / STANDARD LIBRARY COMPARISON</div><h1>Usable std. Mixed performance. Measurable gaps.</h1><p>Working log-report, file-search and HTTP/JSON apps. These are small application workloads, not complete production-product comparisons.</p><div class="cards"><div class="card"><div class="big">123 ms</div><div>Dyn JSONL report</div><div class="small">C/yyjson 12.5 · Rust 40.8 · Go 181.6</div></div><div class="card"><div class="big">6,276 req/s</div><div>Dyn HTTP / JSON</div><div class="small">Go 5,395 · Node 7,815 · Python 4,754</div></div><div class="card"><div class="big">13.9 ms</div><div>Dyn actual-project search</div><div class="small">Rust 3.9 · Go 4.4 · Python 36.3</div></div></div><section class="panel"><div class="selectors"><select id="app" aria-label="Application"><option value="report">JSONL report</option><option value="search">Large text search</option><option value="project">Actual project search</option><option value="http">HTTP JSON service</option></select><select id="metric" aria-label="Metric"></select></div><h2 id="title"></h2><div id="unit" class="small"></div><div id="bars"></div><p id="note" class="small"></p></section><div class="notes"><div><strong>Integer reads matter.</strong> The first Dyn port took 667 ms using general f64 reads. Checked integer reads reduce it to 123 ms, preserving the schema. Both experiments are retained. No compiler/std patch.</div><div><strong>Enough for these Linux apps; not a blanket production stamp.</strong> Found a lazy-arena read edge case and directory portability limits. Search still trails compiled peers. Dyn's sampled four-client HTTP p95 is worse despite competitive throughput.</div></div><div class="footer">Linux x86-64 · one server/CLI CPU · 9 CLI samples after 2 warmups · 5 HTTP rounds, 10,000 checked responses. C uses installed yyjson; Rust uses serde_json; other ports use std. Inputs/outputs match; libraries, runtimes and app designs differ. HTTP uses fresh connections, not keep-alive. No universal language ranking, tail-latency guarantee, or multi-core/production qualification.</div></main><script>const data=CHARTS,names=NAMES;const app=document.querySelector('#app'),metric=document.querySelector('#metric');function draw(){let a=data[app.value],m=a.metrics[metric.value];document.querySelector('#title').textContent=a.label;document.querySelector('#unit').textContent=m.label;document.querySelector('#note').textContent=a.note;let max=Math.max(...Object.values(m.values));document.querySelector('#bars').innerHTML=Object.keys(names).filter(k=>k in m.values).map(k=>`<div class="row ${k}"><div>${names[k]}</div><div class="track"><div class="bar" style="width:${m.values[k]/max*100}%"></div></div><div class="value">${m.values[k].toFixed(metric.value==='rps'?0:2)}</div></div>`).join('')}function pick(){metric.innerHTML=Object.entries(data[app.value].metrics).map(([k,v])=>`<option value="${k}">${v.label.split(' · ')[0]}</option>`).join('');draw()}app.onchange=pick;metric.onchange=draw;pick();</script></body></html>'''.replace('CHARTS',json.dumps(charts)).replace('NAMES',json.dumps(names))
(W/'comparison.html').write_text(html)
print('Wrote',W/'REPORT.md',W/'comparison.html')
